e('Most security bugs are not clever. They are things nobody thought about:','Security problems can arise from overlooked assumptions:','Remove unsupported prevalence claim.')
e('External entity | Outside your control','External entity | Outside the modelled system','External to system need not outside organisation/control.')
e('Data crossing a trust boundary is where most threats live, because one side cannot assume the other side is honest.','Crossing a trust boundary is a useful prompt to check assumptions about identity, permissions and input. Threats can also originate inside a boundary.','No unsupported concentration claim.')
e('The mnemonic is useful because it is a checklist. Teams without one tend to find only the threats they already know about, usually information disclosure, and miss repudiation and denial of service.','The mnemonic prompts consideration of different failure modes, including repudiation and denial of service, alongside disclosure.','Remove unsupported team behaviour prevalence.')
e("Not every threat applies to every element. A data flow can't be \"spoofed\" in the same sense as a user, and a data store doesn't elevate privilege by itself. Microsoft's **STRIDE-per-element** mapping focuses the questions:","Microsoft's classic **STRIDE-per-element** mapping focuses questions on particular elements. It is a modelling heuristic, not proof that other threat categories cannot matter; consider endpoints, supporting processes and the chosen level of abstraction:",'Mapping is a heuristic.',[m])
e('is it HTTPS everywhere, so it can\'t be read or modified in transit?','does correctly validated TLS protect the intended connection endpoints, and where does TLS terminate?','TLS scope and validation assumptions.')
e('- **Queue to worker (T, I)**: can anything other than the web app publish to the queue?','- **Queue to worker (T, I, D)**: who may publish, can messages be altered or disclosed, and can flooding or queue failure prevent delivery?','Include DoS from own mapping.')
e('STRIDE is the most widely taught, but not the only option:','Other approaches include:','Unsupported popularity ranking.')
e('The numbers are attacker cost on a 1 to 10 scale. Compute a node\'s cost from its children:','The numbers are invented additive cost units for this exercise, not measured difficulty or ordinal 1-to-10 ratings. Assume costs do not overlap, all steps are feasible and attackers choose the cheapest route. Under those assumptions:','Ordinal scale cannot meaningfully sum; clarify toy additive model.',[a])
e('That result tells you where to spend effort. Encrypting backups more strongly does nothing for the cheapest path; fixing the missing ownership check does. After that fix, the cheapest path becomes credential stuffing at 3, so MFA and breached-password checks are next.','In this model, removing the IDOR path raises the minimum from 2 to 3. Strengthening a cost-14 path alone leaves that minimum unchanged. Real prioritisation also needs impact, likelihood, mitigation cost and interactions between paths; these invented numbers cannot rank a real deployment.','Do not claim universal prioritisation from unmeasured attacker cost.',[a,cv])
e('You can annotate leaves with other attributes too: probability, need for insider access, detectability. The same min and sum logic works for cost; for "requires special equipment?" an OR node is true only if every child requires it.','Other annotations need their own aggregation rules. For this additive cost model use minimum/sum; for "requires special equipment?", OR requires it only when every feasible alternative does, while AND requires it when any required step does. Probabilities require explicit dependence assumptions and must not simply be added like costs.','Specify aggregation semantics and feasibility.',[a])
e('| Transfer | Someone else holds the risk | Insurer, SaaS provider |','| Transfer/share | Allocate some consequences or responsibility | Insurance or a service agreement; residual risk remains |','Outsourcing does not transfer all consequences.')
e("To prioritise, teams rate **likelihood and impact** (often a simple high, medium, low grid), or use CVSS for known vulnerabilities. Microsoft's old **DREAD** scoring is now rarely recommended because its scores were too subjective to compare.","To prioritise, teams assess **likelihood and impact** with explicit assumptions. CVSS describes vulnerability severity; a Base score alone is not an organisation's complete risk assessment. Historical popularity claims about DREAD are omitted because this review did not establish reliable comparative evidence.",'Distinguish severity and risk, remove unsupported current popularity.',[o,cv])
e('- **Early.** A threat model at design time costs an hour; the same finding after launch costs an incident.','- **Early.** Design-time review can expose issues before deployment; neither review duration nor a later incident is guaranteed.','Unsupported universal timing/cost.')
e('- Attack trees cost a single goal: OR nodes take the minimum, AND nodes the sum; fix the cheapest path first.','- In the additive toy model, OR takes minimum cost and AND takes the sum. Use real evidence and impact to prioritise actual risks.','Constrain model.',[a])
c(2,'explanation','Correct. Internet-to-app, app-to-third-party and user-to-kernel interactions can cross boundaries with different trust assumptions. They are useful review points, but threats also arise within a boundary.','Remove most threats unsupported claim.',option=2)
c(3,'explanation','Correct. The missing attribution evidence leaves a repudiation problem. Protected logs can contribute evidence; session, device and IP records alone do not prove which human authorised an action.','Evidence does not prove human.',option=0)
c(3,'workedExample','The customer may have acted, or another party may have used the account. Missing records make investigation harder. Record appropriate account, event and authentication context in protected logs, avoiding raw session tokens. Strong authentication and independent evidence help, but metadata alone cannot settle every dispute.','Avoid log proof and raw session logging.')
c(4,'workedExample','B2 = 2 + 4 = 6; B = min(9,6) = 6; A = 4 + 3 = 7; root = min(7,6,8) = 6. In this additive model B via B2 is cheapest. Hardening C alone does not change that minimum, though other risk considerations may still justify it.','Correct arithmetic, qualify recommendation.',[a])
c(6,'prompt',"In an additive toy model for reading another customer's invoice, root OR options cost 2 (IDOR), 3 (credential stuffing), and 14 (encrypted-backup theft). Assume each mitigation only affects its named path. Which option raises the minimum attack cost?",'Original asks reduces cost when defender intends increase.',[a])
c(6,'workedExample','Root = min(2,3,14) = 2. Removing the IDOR path leaves min(3,14) = 3 under the stated assumptions. Hardening either other path alone leaves the minimum at 2. These invented units illustrate aggregation; they do not establish actual attack or mitigation costs.','Bound priority result.',[a])
c(6,'explanation','MFA can raise the stuffing-path cost, but the independent IDOR path still costs 2 under the question assumptions.','No universal order.',[a],2)
c(7,'explanation','Correct. Check publisher authentication and publish permissions first. Even the authorised web app can be compromised or submit invalid data, so the worker also needs validation and appropriate authorisation.','Onlypublisher insufficient for trust.',option=0)
c(9,'explanation','Correct. The build and deployment pipeline can influence production code and credentials; include its permissions and any independent deployment checks.','Pipeline compromise not necessarily everyruntimecontrol.',option=0)
c(9,'explanation','Correct. Backups contain sensitive copies and may have different access controls; include them in the model.','Unsupported frequency removed.',option=1)
c(9,'explanation','Correct. Powerful support tools and staff access create relevant data and control flows, including risks from compromised credentials or social engineering.','Unsupported manytakeoversremoved.',option=2)
c(9,'text','A decorative colour palette with no effect on security prompts or data flows','Visual design can matter for securedecisionmaking.',option=3)
c(9,'explanation','This purely decorative choice is outside the described data-flow model. Visual design affecting consent, warnings or security decisions could be relevant.','Do not deny all UIsecurity.',option=3)
c(9,'workedExample','Include flows beyond requests: build artifacts and deployment credentials; backup and log copies; support tools with sensitive permissions. A pipeline compromise can undermine production, but does not necessarily bypass independent runtime controls. Review each component and its boundaries.','Remove universal bypass.')
